  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to use a **multi-scale optimization with curvature-based shifts** combined with **polynomial spectral basis** representations. Unlike previous surface-following methods, this approach:\n\n- Uses a fixed 64-point solution as a base\n- Represents functions on larger grids using Smolyak sparse grids\n- Projects onto low-discrepancy point sets using a potential flow formulation\n- Solves the optimization problem using a convex projection of the correlation coefficients\n\nThe rationale is that the Erd\u0151s discrepancy function often exhibits smooth behavior that can be better captured by polynomial expansions and leveraging the fact that optimal solutions tend to have low effective dimension.\n\nThe key limitations are:\n- Requires solving a large system (~10^6 variables)\n- Potential numerical issues with large grids\n- Higher gradient evaluation cost (O(n\u00b3))\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nProblem Representation:\n\nLet grid spacing dx=2/n, vectors h_n = [h(x\u2081),...,h(x\u2099)] \u2208 \u211d\n  \n\u03c9 = (\u03c9\u2080,\u03c9\u2081,...,\u03c9\u208b\u2081) with \u2211\u03c9\u1d62=0\nGoal: Minimize ||corr(h_n)||\u221e \n\nConstraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 \u2192 \u2211h_i * dx = 1\n\nSpectral Representation:\n\nWe use a 5-term Legendre polynomial basis expansion:\n\np(x) = a\u2080 + a\u2081*x*cos(\u03c0x/2) + a\u2082*(3(\u03c0x/2)^2/6 - 1) + a\u2083*(5(\u03c0x/2)^4/120 - 4\u03c0x\u00b2/36 + 1/3) + a\u2084*sin(\u03c0x)\n\nEach basis function is normalized differently to account for their varying range.\n\nConstraint Enforcement:\n\n1. Integral constraint: Solve \u2211c\u1d62 * \u222bp\u1d62(x) dx = n/2 (after normalization)\n2. Boundedness: Project coefficients (0 \u2264 c\u1d62 \u2264 Cmax)\n\nObjective Function:\n\nF(c) = max_k ||corr(\u2211c\u1d62*p\u1d62)||\u221e\n\nThe correlation is computed using Legendre basis coefficients under the change of variables.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nPhase 1: Basis Construction (Fixed)\n  For n in [64, 128, 256, 512]:\n    Define grid points and Legendre coefficients\n    Scale and prepare the basis\n\nPhase 2: Optimization\n  Solve minimize F(c) subject to \u2211c\u1d62*a\u1d62 = n/2 and c\u1d62 bounds.\n\n  Steps:\n    - Compute grid correlation I(k) = \u222bh(x)h(x+k)dx\n    - Express F(c) using basis expansion\n    - Use projected gradient descent with curvature-based shifts\n    - Apply cutoffs for convergence\n\nPhase 3: Many-Start Optimization\n  Initialize from:\n    - Thue-Morse shifted by \u03c0/4\n    - Random candidate with convex hull projection\n    - Previous best solution (64-point)\n\n  Hyperparameters:\n    - Projection learning rate \u03b1\n    - Maximum gradient norm G_max = 0.8\n    - Vanishing schedule for gradient component \u03b2=0.5^{1/3000}\n\n4. Search Choices\n\nInitializations:\n  - 3 heuristics: Thue-Morse, binary balance, and parent solution\n  - Each evolved through 30 least-angle projection steps\n\nProjections:\n  - Following gradient: L2 penalty vs. Box constraint\n  - With curvature: Add diagonal perturbations based on loss curvature\n\nStopping:\n  - Nested condition on |gradient| < 10^{-4} and progress < 1e-5\n  - Allowed inner iterations: After 2\u00d7coarse grid time\n\n5. Numerical Robustness\n\n  - Handle loss floating point by re-normalization\n  - Constrained solution recovery via:\n    * Least squares projection\n    * Feature clipping matrix\n  - Fallback to solution with smallest recorded C5\n\n6. Runtime Safeguards\n\n  - Allocate n_points (2^10 to 2^12)\n  - Start and time each heuristic\n  - Compute loss only if occupied grid>95%\n  - Terminate if any parameter exceeds bounds\n\n  Fallback procedure:\n\nfreq = {k: cand_list[k]} ;\nif 'times' not in freq.resources: \n    grad = compute_gradients(cand_list['current'])\n    cand_list = {'current': current_vector}\nreturn cand_list['current']",
